<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/brick-mill.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/brick-mill-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/brick-mill.webp 1018w" sizes="100vw" width="1018" height="679" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Dover</span></nav><p class="kicker">Seacoast · Strafford County</p><h1>Dover, NH Insurance Appraiser</h1><p class="lede">Dover is a mill city on the Cocheco that has quietly become one of the Seacoast’s busiest places to live. Its claim disputes reflect that – brick lofts, old wood houses and brand-new construction all on the same storm map.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Strafford County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Seacoast</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>Cocheco mill lofts, Victorian homes and newer subdivisions</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> Garrison Hill, the downtown mill district, Bellamy, Sawyer Mills.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> The May 2006 Mother’s Day floods sent rivers across Strafford County over their banks, and Dover-area homeowners learned the hard way how differently flood and water-damage coverage work.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>River flooding</li><li>Nor’easters</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul></div>

<h2>Mill brick and wood frame on the Cocheco</h2>
<p>The Cocheco River falls through downtown Dover, and the old Cocheco Mills that grew up along it now hold apartments, offices and shops. Up the hill, Garrison Hill and the streets around it are lined with Victorian and early-20th-century houses, many with original woodwork. Toward Bellamy, newer subdivisions and condo developments keep going up as the city grows.</p>
<p>Each of those housing types breaks in its own way.</p>
<h2>Three kinds of Dover claim</h2>
<h3>Loft and mill-building units</h3>
<p>Mill conversions have thick masonry, timber framing and big windows. Water losses that start in one unit tend to migrate through old floor systems into units below, and the association’s master policy and each owner’s unit policy have to be lined up. When everyone agrees what is covered but not what it costs, appraisal is usually available. See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">commercial and HOA appraisal</a>.</p>
<h3>Older single-family homes</h3>
<p>Ice dams on complicated Victorian rooflines, slate and old flashing, plaster walls – the usual pattern is an insurer’s estimate written for a simpler, newer house. The fix is a careful scope that reflects what is actually there. Our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/ice-dam-roof-snow-appraisal/">ice-dam appraisal</a> page covers the common gaps.</p>
<h3>Newer construction</h3>
<p>Newer homes raise different arguments: whether a partial roof or siding repair can match, whether manufacturer specs require full-slope replacement, and how depreciation is calculated on materials only a few years old.</p>
<h2>Water: the coverage line to know</h2>
<p>Dover’s rivers and low spots make flooding a real concern. Most homeowners policies exclude surface-water flooding, while sudden water from inside the house – a burst supply line, an overflowing appliance – is often covered. Groundwater seepage, sewer backup and sump failure each sit in their own corner of the policy and may need an endorsement. Before you argue about the price of water damage, make sure which part of the policy you are under.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Before you tear out</b>Photograph wet areas, moisture lines and damaged materials, and keep a sample of flooring and trim. Those details often decide the scope later.</div>
<p>The Sawyer Mills complex on the Bellamy River and the downtown Cocheco buildings are reminders that much of Dover’s character is built in brick. Masonry repairs – repointing, rebuilding parapets, replacing damaged lintels – call for specialized trades and lime-compatible mortars in older walls. If an estimate prices a brick repair the way it would price vinyl siding, the gap can be substantial.</p>
<h2>How we help Dover owners</h2>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser reviews insurer estimates line by line and, when you invoke the appraisal clause, serves as your appointed appraiser. We also act as neutral umpires. We are not attorneys; legal questions about coverage or deadlines go to a New Hampshire lawyer. Check where your claim stands with the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/nh-appraisal-readiness-score/">appraisal readiness score</a>, or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact us</a>.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in Dover</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in Dover and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by Dover owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My downstairs neighbor’s ceiling was damaged by my leak. Whose policy pays?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the association’s documents and both unit policies. Often the association’s master policy handles common elements, and unit policies handle improvements and contents. Get copies of all three before arguing amounts.</p></div></details><details><summary>The river came up into my basement. Can appraisal help?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If your homeowners policy excludes flood, appraisal can’t create coverage. If you carry flood insurance, read that policy’s own dispute provisions, which differ from homeowners forms.</p></div></details></div>
